title:
Weird SELECT view when a record is modified to the same value by two transactions

body:
## Bug Report

Please answer these questions before submitting your issue. Thanks!

### 1. Minimal reproduce step (Required)
```sql
/* init */ create table t(a int, b int);
/* init */ insert into t values (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2);

/* s1 */ begin;
/* s1 */ select * from t; -- [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s2 */ begin;
/* s2 */ update t set a = 10 where b = 1;
/* s2 */ commit;
/* s1 */ select * from t; -- [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s1 */ update t set a = 10 where true;
/* s1 */ select * from t;  -- [(10, 0), (1, 1), (10, 2)]
/* s1 */ commit;
```
### 2. What did you expect to see? (Required)

The result of final SELECT should be `(10, 0), (10, 1), (10, 2)`.

### 3. What did you see instead (Required)

The result of final SELECT is `(10, 0), (1, 1), (10, 2)`.

I think it is so weird for session 1 to see the second row is still `(1, 1)` after the successful execution of an `UPDATE` with the `WHERE TRUE` predicate. 

So I think it will be better for s1 to see all records it updates regardless of whether the values before and after the UPDATE are the same.


### 4. What is your TiDB version? (Required)

```
+--------------------+
| version()          |
+--------------------+
| 5.7.25-TiDB-v5.2.1 |
+--------------------+
```

comments:
[issue_comment | you06 | 2021-09-22T06:32:13Z]
I think this may be a weird behavior of mixed usage of snapshot read and current read. IMO, there is no unified sound solution for this problem, our goal is just choosing a relatively reasonable one.

My analysis:

```sql
/* s1 */ begin;
/* s1 */ select * from t; -- [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s2 */ begin;
/* s2 */ update t set a = 10 where b = 1;
/* s2 */ commit; -- the latest values are [(0, 0), (10, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s1 */ select * from t; -- [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)]
/* s1 */ update t set a = 10 where true; -- 2 rows affected
-- Update statement will go through current read, which will read the latest rows [(0, 0), (10, 1), (2, 2)].
-- After read the rows, the update is applied on the them, [(0, 0), (10, 1), (2, 2)] -> [(10, 0), (10, 1), (10, 2)].
-- Notice that the row (10, 1) is not changed by this statement,
-- so this is not in it's changed buffer(can be accessed by internal read).

/* s1 */ select * from t;  -- [(10, 0), (1, 1), (10, 2)]
-- This read statement read will try reading snapshot rows and changed rows,
-- then combine them,
-- since (10, 1) is not in changed rows, it's not visible to the transaction.

/* s1 */ commit;
```

If you test this case with mysql 8, the same result will be returned. Discussion is welcome.

---COMMENT---

[issue_comment | TrocTool | 2021-09-22T09:01:44Z]
I think you are right. It may be an implementation concession but I think it can introduce serious logic errors to upper application systems under certain circumstance. 

Can we add the row (10, 1) into the changed buffer even the values before and after the UPDATE are the same?

By the way, we have noticed that MySQL has the same problem and has submitted a same issue to MySQL team. Please see:
https://bugs.mysql.com/bug.php?id=104986

